From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: explorer: the Windows key's shortcuts

The Windows key did nothing on its own, and none of its everyday shortcuts
existed.

- The Windows key alone opens Start (on release, and only if no other key
  was pressed while it was down -- a low-level keyboard hook).
- Win+D shows the desktop and, pressed again, puts the windows back;
  Win+M minimizes all, Win+Shift+M restores them.
- Win+Left/Right snap the active window to half its monitor's work area,
  and back from the other side; Win+Up maximizes; Win+Down restores a
  maximized or snapped window, and minimizes a normal one.
- Win+E opens File Explorer, Win+R the Run dialog (shell32's RunFileDlg, on
  its own thread), Win+I the settings (the Control Panel), Win+Pause
  System, Win+X the quick-link menu (Apps and Features, System, Network
  Connections, PowerShell, Task Manager, Settings, Control Panel, File
  Explorer, Run, Desktop).

All act on the current virtual desktop's windows (0068).

Gate: test/shellkeys-gate.sh, typed on the X keyboard.
